---
/**
 * Who Axcess is for: one section per kind of reader, then the jobs Axcess is
 * the wrong tool for.
 *
 * Order follows the work: the accessibility lead who runs the scans, the
 * editors and developers who receive the fixes, the leaders who fund the
 * work, and the IT reviewers who approve the tool. Each section says who the
 * reader is, the job in their words, and what they get, so a reader can find
 * their own section from the "On this page" list and skip the rest (COGA
 * "Making Content Usable": help users find what they need; SC 2.4.6
 * Headings and Labels, Level AA).
 *
 * The persona sections are plain prose with Starlight's article styles,
 * not a two-column card layout as on the old page: lists read best in one
 * column at every width, and nothing has to reflow at 320 px (SC 1.4.10
 * Reflow, Level AA). The old page's sticky left column is gone too, since
 * sticky content can cover focused items (SC 2.4.11 Focus Not Obscured
 * (Minimum), Level AA).
 *
 * Copy changed from the old page where the code says otherwise (see the
 * comments beside each list).
 */
import StarlightPage from "@astrojs/starlight/components/StarlightPage.astro";
import Card from "../components/Card.astro";
import { url } from "../lib/site";

const headings = [
  { depth: 2, slug: "accessibility-lead", text: "The accessibility lead" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "editors-and-developers", text: "The editor or developer who makes the fix" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "leadership", text: "Leaders and compliance owners" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "it-and-security", text: "IT and security reviewers" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "not-designed-for", text: "Where Axcess is the wrong tool" },
];
---

<StarlightPage
  frontmatter={{
    title: "Who Axcess is for",
    description:
      "Accessibility leads, the editors and developers who fix things, leaders, and IT reviewers: what each of them gets from Axcess.",
  }}
  headings={headings}
>
  <p>
    Axcess was made for an accessibility lead at a large university, and designed around the people that lead hands work
    to. Find yourself below to see what you would get.
  </p>

  <h2 id="accessibility-lead">The accessibility lead</h2>
  <p>
    You are responsible for dozens of sites. You sort through hundreds of issues in one sitting, often while sharing your
    screen with an editor who has never seen the tool.
  </p>
  <!-- The job statement is a paraphrase of the persona's goal
       (docs/internal/personas.md), not a quote from a real person, so it is
       labelled "The job" rather than set as a quotation. -->
  <p class="job"><strong>The job:</strong> find every barrier across a site before the next report, hand a list in priority order to the content team, and check the fixes.</p>
  <h3>What you get</h3>
  <!-- Changed from the old page: no "one-keystroke decisions" (number keys
       work only on an image's own page, routes/FindingDetail.tsx), no "who
       decided" (status history stores no person's identity), and no manual
       review matrix in the app for ordinary scans (the old
       /scans/:id/manual-checks route now redirects, App.tsx); the written
       report's checklist replaces it (exports/audit_report.py). -->
  <ul>
    <li>A grouped issue table, sorted by priority, as soon as a scan finishes. It shows only that report's results.</li>
    <li>For each issue: how sure Axcess is (Mostly sure, Not sure or For information), the abilities it affects, such as vision, and the evidence, side by side.</li>
    <li>
      A status for every occurrence: New, Reviewing, In progress, Fixed, Accepted risk or Not a problem. The last four
      need a short reason, kept with the report.
    </li>
    <li>
      For Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) criteria that no check covers, a list in the written report of what
      a person needs to test.
    </li>
    <li>A comparison with an earlier report, so you spend your time on what changed.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Designed for you</h3>
  <p>
    The app aims for WCAG 2.2 Level AAA where it can, with large targets, strong text contrast and plain language. It works
    fully by keyboard, fits a phone-width screen, and never shows how serious a problem is by color alone. If you use a
    screen reader, magnification or only a keyboard, Axcess was built with you in mind.
  </p>

  <h2 id="editors-and-developers">The editor or developer who makes the fix</h2>
  <p>You may never open Axcess. You get a ticket, a spreadsheet row or a report, and you need to know what to change.</p>
  <p class="job"><strong>The job:</strong> take the issue, fix the page, mark it done, and move on.</p>
  <h3>What you get</h3>
  <!-- Changed from the old page: "grouped by cause" is not true; Axcess
       groups occurrences by the check that found them (docs/glossary.md
       "Root cause"). The Jira file is a command-line export only
       (cli.py --format jira; the app's Export menu lists four formats,
       ExportMenu.tsx). -->
  <ul>
    <li>Plain-language steps to fix each issue, written for the person making the change.</li>
    <li>The exact page, the element locator (CSS selector) and the element code (HTML), so there is no hunting.</li>
    <li>A "Done when" note, so you know when the fix is complete.</li>
    <li>An Excel issue list with links you can click, and a file you can import into Jira from the command line.</li>
    <li>Every page an issue appears on, in one place, so you can spot a problem that comes from one shared template.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="leadership">Leaders and compliance owners</h2>
  <p>You need to know how exposed a site is, what to fund first, and whether last quarter's work made a difference.</p>
  <p class="job"><strong>The job:</strong> know how well the site was checked, what to fix first, and whether it is getting better.</p>
  <h3>What you get</h3>
  <!-- Changed from the old page: there is no "Who's Affected" view in the
       app. Each issue lists who it affects ("Affects", IssueEvidence.tsx),
       and the written report has a "Who is affected" section
       (audit_report.py). Priority combines impact with spread
       (docs/glossary.md "Priority"). -->
  <ul>
    <li>A written report that starts with what was checked: the start page, the WCAG version and level, the pages and the checks used.</li>
    <li>Issues ranked by priority, which combines how badly a problem affects people with how many pages it is on.</li>
    <li>A "Who is affected" section that counts the issues affecting each ability: vision, hearing, movement (motor) and thinking (cognition).</li>
    <li>A plain list of which WCAG criteria Axcess's checks help with, and which a person still needs to test.</li>
    <li>A trend chart across reports, instead of a new number each time that can't be compared.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>What you won't get</h3>
  <p>
    A certificate. Axcess never makes a claim that a site meets WCAG, and never writes an Accessibility Conformance
    Report (ACR) for you. A qualified person writes those, using Axcess evidence.
  </p>

  <h2 id="it-and-security">IT and security reviewers</h2>
  <p>You have to approve a tool that visits your organization's websites, some of them behind a sign-in.</p>
  <p class="job"><strong>The job:</strong> know where the data goes, what the tool connects to, and what happens to passwords.</p>
  <h3>What you get</h3>
  <!-- Changed from the old page: added the desktop app's update check
       (desktop/src/updates.cjs, off with AXCESS_DISABLE_UPDATE_CHECK=1,
       desktop/src/main.cjs). Dropped "login pages are not stored as
       evidence", which the code could not confirm. The stricter setup is
       the protected-scan deployment (docs/hosting.md;
       protected_scans_enabled defaults to false in src/audit/config.py). -->
  <ul>
    <li>
      No cloud service, no account and no usage tracking (telemetry). In the desktop app, reports live in a database in the
      folder the computer keeps for app data.
    </li>
    <li>
      Connections only to the site being scanned, and to a local AI model on the same computer if you install one. The
      desktop app also asks GitHub once each time it opens whether a newer version exists. That request carries no scan
      data, and you can turn it off.
    </li>
    <li>Passwords never enter Axcess. The person scanning signs in themselves, in a browser window they can see.</li>
    <li>A desktop app that runs in a locked-down window (sandbox) with a written security boundary, and source code you can read.</li>
    <li>
      A separate, stricter setup for sensitive sign-in scans across an organization, with your organization's sign-in,
      encrypted evidence and automatic deletion after seven days. It is off by default, and IT has to set it up.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p><a href={url("/privacy/")}>Read the full privacy and trust page.</a></p>

  <h2 id="not-designed-for">Where Axcess is the wrong tool</h2>
  <!-- Three equal cards in a grid: three separate "no" answers of the same
       kind, so the same card component as the rest of the site (SC 3.2.4
       Consistent Identification, Level AA). The grid falls to one column
       at narrow widths (SC 1.4.10 Reflow, Level AA). -->
  <div class="not-content wrong-tool">
    <Card heading="Public scanning services">
      <p>
        Axcess runs on one computer, for one person or a small trusted team. It is not a web service anyone can point at
        any site, and it must never be open to the public internet.
      </p>
    </Card>
    <Card heading="Nightly checks of many sites">
      <p>
        You can run scans on a schedule from the command line. But the app is built for careful review of one report at a
        time, not for dashboards across many sites.
      </p>
    </Card>
    <Card heading="Automatic certification">
      <p>No scan proves a site meets WCAG. Testing with people who use assistive technology is still essential.</p>
    </Card>
  </div>
</StarlightPage>

<style>
  /* The job line: a tinted band so it reads as a summary, set apart by a
     border and a bold label as well as the tint, so it does not rely on
     colour (SC 1.4.1 Use of Color, Level A). */
  .job {
    padding: 0.875rem 1.125rem;
    border-inline-start: 4px solid var(--ax-border-strong);
    border-radius: 0 0.5rem 0.5rem 0;
    background: var(--ax-surface-subtle);
    color: var(--ax-fg);
  }

  .wrong-tool {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 12rem), 1fr));
    gap: 1rem;
    margin-top: 1.25rem;
  }
</style>
